<header><p class="enc-kicker">Event · disaster</p><h1 class="enc-title">Hurricane Maria</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/2017">2017</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/21st-century">21st century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Hurricane Maria was a Category 5 hurricane that struck the eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caribbean">Caribbean</a> in September 2017, two weeks after <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hurricane-irma">Hurricane Irma</a>. It caused catastrophic damage on Dominica and then crossed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/puerto-rico">Puerto Rico</a>, destroying its power grid; studies estimated around 3,000 deaths there in the following months. It is among the deadliest Atlantic hurricanes of modern times.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/hurricane-maria.jpg" alt="Hurricane Maria" width="320" height="407" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: NASA Worldview · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Maria_2017-09-20_0238Z_(Coastline-less).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Course of events</h2><p>Maria grew from a tropical wave that left the west coast of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/africa">Africa</a> on 12 September 2017. It became Tropical Depression Fifteen at 12:00 UTC on 16 September, about 665 miles (1,070 km) east of Barbados, and a tropical storm six hours later. Warm seas of 29 °C, low wind shear and abundant moisture let it intensify explosively on 18 September. It reached Category 5 strength just before making landfall on Dominica at 21:15 AST that night, with sustained winds of 165 mph (266 km/h).</p><p>After crossing Dominica the storm re-intensified and peaked with winds of 175 mph (280 km/h) and a central pressure of 908 millibars. An eyewall replacement cycle weakened it to a high-end Category 4 hurricane, and it struck <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/puerto-rico">Puerto Rico</a> near Yabucoa on 20 September with winds of 155 mph (249 km/h). It then passed north of the Bahamas, paralleled the United States East Coast, and became extratropical on 30 September before dissipating by 2 October.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Aftermath and significance</h2><p>On Dominica the winds damaged the roof of practically every home, including the prime minister's official residence, and cut off cellular, radio and internet services, so that the scale of the damage was unclear for days. Guadeloupe and Martinique suffered flooding, damaged roofs and uprooted trees, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saint-croix">Saint Croix</a> in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states-virgin-islands">United States Virgin Islands</a> was crossed by the hurricane's outer eyewall two weeks after <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hurricane-irma">Hurricane Irma</a> had struck St Thomas and St John.</p><p>In <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/puerto-rico">Puerto Rico</a> the eye had tripled in size before landfall, exposing a far greater area to high winds. Rainfall peaked at 37.9 inches (960 mm) at Caguas, San Juan flooded and the La Perla neighbourhood was largely destroyed. The power grid was effectively destroyed. On 26 September 95 percent of the island had no electricity and less than half the population had tap water. The total death toll was 3,059, of which an estimated 2,975 were in Puerto Rico and 65 in Dominica.</p><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a> government response was criticised as slow and inadequate. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/departement">Department</a> of Homeland Security did not at once waive the Jones Act, which limited aid shipments from United States ports to ships carrying the United States flag, and waived it for ten days from 28 September after Governor Ricardo Rosselló asked. The state power company awarded a 300 million dollar repair contract to Whitefish Energy, a small <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/montana">Montana</a> firm, which prompted accusations of cronyism that Interior Secretary Ryan Zinke denied.</p><p>Governor Rosselló estimated the damage at no less than 90 billion dollars. Maria was the deadliest hurricane in Dominica since 1834 and in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/puerto-rico">Puerto Rico</a> since 1899. The World Meteorological Organization retired the name in April 2018 and replaced it with Margot.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Hurricane Maria” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote><b>Hurricane Maria</b> caused enormous damage in 2017, and Dominica, which funds much of its development through citizenship by investment, aims to become a climate-resilient nation.</blockquote><a href="/state/commonwealth-of-dominica">Commonwealth of Dominica</a></li><li><blockquote>A debt crisis led Congress to impose a fiscal oversight board in 2016, and <b>Hurricane Maria</b> devastated the island in 2017.</blockquote><a href="/state/puerto-rico-united-states">Puerto Rico (United States)</a></li></ul></section>
